{
  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-144343",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "First, notice that the transformation $Y={X}^{2}+1$ is two-to-one over the entire interval $-2<x<2.$ \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14231916/assets/8a03bd46aa6d415d8e1a49ff74557886702c1dc4bc9164ce2a5a1252874e1b4e.png)\n\nMoreover, for $-2<x<2,$  we have that $Y$ is nonzero on $1\\le y<5.$ \n\nNow, using the definition of the cumulative distribution of $Y,$ for $1\\le y<5,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}_{Y}(y) & =P(Y\\le y) \\\\  & =P({X}^{2}+1\\le y) \\\\  & =P(-(y-1)^{1/2}\\le X\\le (y-1)^{1/2}) \\\\  & ={F}_{X}((y-1)^{1/2})-{F}_{X}(-(y-1)^{1/2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo compute the PDF ${f}_{Y}(y)$ for $1\\le y<5,$ we differentiate ${F}_{Y}$ with respect to $y$ using the chain rule. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & ={F}_{Y}^{′}(y) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(y-1)^{-1/2}\\cdot {F}_{X}^{′}((y-1)^{1/2})+\\frac{1}{2}(y-1)^{-1/2}\\cdot {F}_{X}^{′}(-(y-1)^{1/2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(y-1)^{-1/2}\\cdot {f}_{X}((y-1)^{1/2})+\\frac{1}{2}(y-1)^{-1/2}\\cdot {f}_{X}(-(y-1)^{1/2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(y-1)^{-1/2}[{f}_{X}((y-1)^{1/2})+{f}_{X}(-(y-1)^{1/2})].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the definition of ${f}_{X},$ we finally arrive at\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & =\\frac{1}{2}(y-1)^{-1/2}[{f}_{X}((y-1)^{1/2})+{f}_{X}(-(y-1)^{1/2})] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(y-1)^{-1/2}[\\frac{3}{16}{((y-1)^{1/2})}^{2}+\\frac{3}{16}{(-(y-1)^{1/2})}^{2}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(y-1)^{-1/2}(\\frac{3}{16}(y-1)+\\frac{3}{16}(y-1)) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{16}(y-1)^{1/2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{16}\\sqrt{y-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the full expression for the PDF of $Y$ is\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{16}\\sqrt{y-1}, & 1\\le y<5 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
